====================================================================================================
VERDICT
====================================================================================================
  0. THE DATA UNDERNEATH THIS SCRIPT WAS WRONG AND IS NOW SOURCED, and the 'was' column is
     gone. The four cutoff values and the +/- 2.0 km/s bar are withdrawn as unsourceable;
     the banked 8.7/6.6/3.2/1.7 sigma comparison points are withdrawn with them rather than
     rescaled, because their redshifts do not exist in the replacement table.
  1. MY DIRECTION WAS RIGHT; MY MAGNITUDE WAS NOT. I told Carl this lever 'runs the
     framework's way'. It does -- by 8% in x, i.e. -0.04 sigma -- not by the factor
     needed. And the reason it is small is a symmetry I should have quoted up front:
     g = -grad Phi is a FIRST derivative while delta ~ grad^2 Phi is a SECOND, so for a
     Gaussian field <g_i delta> = 0 BY PARITY. S2 confirms that numerically -- every bin
     sits in the range 0.9979-1.0005 of the global rms.
     Any density dependence is therefore PURELY NONLINEAR, and hence necessarily modest at
     the mild overdensities where the forest lives. That was predictable without a field.
  2. Nonlinearly (lognormal) the ratio rises with density, giving 1.082 averaged over the
     forest-relevant range. Real, correctly signed, negligible. Per-bin values in S3.
  2b. I ALSO CONSIDERED THE OPPOSITE SIGN and it did not happen: high-delta regions sit near
     potential minima where grad Phi -> 0, which could have made the conditional acceleration
     LOWER and the constraint HARDER. The field says no -- the peak-suppression effect is
     sub-dominant to the general proximity-to-mass effect at these densities.
  3. CONSEQUENCE: the constraint stays essentially where mi_forest_total_acceleration left
     it -- 1.0-8.5 sigma statistical, 0.4-0.9 sigma against the method
     systematic. The last lever I flagged as favourable is effectively NULL.

  WHAT NOW STANDS, with the levers exhausted:
   * forest constraint on the POINTWISE diffuse-sector reading: 1.0-8.5 sigma on Hiss's
     own statistical bars, 0.4-0.9 sigma once the cutoff-fitting method systematic
     that Hiss et al. document in their Sec. 5.3 is carried, across all eight Hiss bins,
     robust against the a0 footing forks, against the peculiar/Hubble split, against the
     total-acceleration treatment, and now against the density conditioning;
   * but NOT uniformly strong once that systematic is carried -- [2.0, 2.2, 2.4, 2.6, 2.8, 3.0, 3.2, 3.4] falls below 3 sigma.
   * TWO caveats survive. (a) The b budget is LCDM-hydro-calibrated, so a self-consistent MI
     hydro simulation is needed to finalise; Arnold, Puchwein & Springel 2015 (MNRAS 448,
     2275) independently document that the forest is systematics-limited at the ~10% level
     for ANY model, GR included. (b) The framework has not closed whether nu's argument is
     the LOCAL peculiar field (tested here) or the cosmological/EFE field, and on the EFE
     branch the predicted effect is a few percent -- below f(R)'s <10% and untestable here.
   * the missing regulator for x -> 0 stays MANDATORY on the pointwise branch.
